HOW REGISTERS ENTER MEMORY AND RETURN FROM A STACK.

English Major C ARMv7-A T32 architecture document.
Status: experimental source specification, not a proven simulator.
This file elaborates precisely the SEVEN distinct newly permitted
operations numbered 54 through 60 in the closed initial grant:
"All and only these initial operations are permitted.english".
It does not admit any other ARM encoding by implication.
Every ARM operation here is unprivileged and in T32 state.

Normative architectural pin:
  ARM DDI 0406C.d, T32 decoding and PUSH/POP, LDM/STM,
  ADD SP and SUB SP descriptions.
Accessible corroboration:
  ARM Cortex-A Series (ARMv7-A) Programmer's Guide DEN0013,
  "Multiple transfers" and "Procedure Call Standard".
  ARM's Instruction Set Assembly Guide 100076_0200_00_en,
  C2.45 (LDM), C2.73 (POP), C2.74 (PUSH), C2.138 (STM).
  ARM's 16-bit Thumb instruction Quick Reference Card.
No source here substitutes an ARMv7-M exception-return rule
for the ARMv7-A application-program instruction semantics.

PART ONE. THE OBJECTS TO WHICH THESE OPERATIONS APPLY.

There is one 32-bit architectural stack pointer register R13.
An application that follows an ordinary ARM full-descending
stack convention treats its SP value as the address of the
currently highest-occupied (lowest-addressed) stack word.

SP is a register containing 32 bits, NOT a stack object in DRAM.
A PUSH transfers values FROM registers TO memory and changes SP.
A POP transfers values FROM memory TO registers and changes SP.
Changing SP without performing a PUSH or POP does not move,
erase, initialize, or allocate physical memory.

The chosen memory model is byte-addressed, with an actual
virtual-memory mapping and access-permission contract that
must be supplied by the environment. An architectural
word transfer addresses FOUR bytes, not four abstract
elements of unknown size.

For the narrow T32 encodings admitted here, every ordinary
register in a register list is one of R0 through R7.
The restricted PUSH can additionally include LR.
The restricted POP can additionally include PC.
A general LDMIA or STMIA has a base Rn chosen from R0
through R7 and an explicitly nonempty register set from
R0 through R7. This language draft additionally requires
that the base not be in that set. That last condition is
a deliberate restriction of the language profile, not a
claim that every ARM encoding with base overlap is invalid.

The order of English names in the human-written register
list MUST NOT choose the order of the memory transfers.
A register set is an unordered set of selected register
numbers. The architectural convention associates the
LOWEST numbered selected register with the LOWEST
address in the accessed interval.

For example, the sets {R3,R0} and {R0,R3}, if permitted,
select exactly the same register numbers and exactly the
same two memory addresses. They do not request reversal
of memory order. Duplicating a member name does not
create an additional register or make the CPU transfer
the value twice.

PART TWO. THE COMPLETE SUCCESSFUL EFFECT OF A PUSH.

Define OLD_STACK_POINTER to be the original SP, read
before the transfer.
Define SELECTED_REGISTERS to be the nonempty set of
R0..R7 and optionally LR selected by the T1 PUSH encoding.
Define COUNT as its number of distinct selected registers.
Define RESERVED_BYTES = 4*COUNT.
Define FIRST_ADDRESS = OLD_STACK_POINTER - RESERVED_BYTES,
with 32-bit address arithmetic where relevant.

Before an attempted successful push, check the address
alignment, data access validity and permission conditions
required by the exact architecture and target environment.

The word at FIRST_ADDRESS receives the old value of
the lowest numbered selected register.
The word at FIRST_ADDRESS+4 receives the old value of
the next higher numbered selected register, if any.
Continue until every selected register has a separate
word, taking old LR as the highest numbered entry
whenever LR is selected.
After normal completion SP equals FIRST_ADDRESS.

The selected source registers retain their values.
N, Z, C, and V do not change. The execution index
normally advances by two bytes. No return address is
created by PUSH; a pushed LR value is merely the
old value of LR copied into memory.

Example: old SP=0x00001000, R0=0x11223344,
R3=0xAABBCCDD, LR=0x00002001, with all affected
stack words writable and word aligned.

PUSH of {R0,R3,LR} has COUNT=3 and FIRST_ADDRESS=0x0FF4.
The stored memory words are:
  address 0x0FF4 receives 0x11223344;
  address 0x0FF8 receives 0xAABBCCDD;
  address 0x0FFC receives 0x00002001.
SP becomes 0x0FF4. R0/R3/LR and flags are preserved.

This example describes word values. An actual byte-by-byte
image must additionally declare effective endianness.
For ordinary little-endian memory, the four bytes at
0x0FF4..0x0FF7 are 0x44, 0x33, 0x22, 0x11 in that order.
Do NOT reverse the register-to-address association when
changing endianness.

PART THREE. THE COMPLETE SUCCESSFUL EFFECT OF A POP.

Define OLD_STACK_POINTER to be the original SP.
Define SELECTED_REGISTERS to be a nonempty set of R0..R7,
optionally with the additional PC bit as allowed by the
chosen narrow POP encoding. The bit for PC is distinct from
bits selecting ordinary low registers.

For normal execution, each selected low register receives
one word at successive addresses, beginning at old SP,
in ascending register-number order. If the PC bit is
selected, the final, highest addressed word is loaded
as a BRANCH TARGET and cannot be treated merely as
another 32-bit general-register datum.

After normal completion SP becomes
OLD_STACK_POINTER + 4*COUNT, where COUNT includes PC
if it was selected. POP does not otherwise change
N/Z/C/V, and it does not directly write LR.

For a POP without PC, ordinary sequential execution
proceeds at the instruction following the POP.
For POP with PC, execution transfers to the target
encoded in the loaded word, subject to its state tag,
alignment, permitted instruction boundary and executable
access. Its successor is NOT necessarily the next
instruction by address.

Example: old SP=0x0FF4, the three memory words
are 0x11223344, 0xAABBCCDD, 0x00002001 at
0x0FF4, 0x0FF8, 0x0FFC respectively.

POP {R0,R3,PC} yields R0=0x11223344,
R3=0xAABBCCDD, SP=0x1000, with the final
loaded word 0x2001 requesting T32 branch target
0x2000. It is NOT equivalent to moving the
integer 0x2001 into an ordinary R15 variable
while continuing at the next instruction.
This example is only successful if 0x2000 is a
permitted executable T32 instruction boundary.

The PUSH example stores LR but a later POP
may restore PC. The register numbers are not
the same: this is intentional and expresses
a common calling/return convention. That
convention is not a promise that every function
has a matching stack frame.

PART FOUR. WHAT A BASE REGISTER MEANS FOR STMIA.

Operation 57 uses a low general-purpose base
register Rn rather than implicitly using SP.
The 16-bit T32 instruction encoding begins with
binary 11000, followed by a three-bit register
number Rn, followed by an eight-bit register-set
mask. It occupies exactly TWO bytes.

Let oldBase be the value of Rn at the start.
Let count be the number of selected register bits.
For successful execution, the first word at oldBase
receives the starting value of the lowest-numbered
selected register; the next selected register's
starting value is written at oldBase+4; continue
until all selected registers are stored.
The base Rn is then assigned oldBase+4*count.

Example: R4=0x3000, R0=0x11223344,
R3=0x55667788, with valid writable mapped memory.
The actual 16-bit T32 instruction encoding 0xC409
is STMIA R4!, {R0,R3}. It writes 0x11223344
to 0x3000, 0x55667788 to 0x3004, then
changes R4 from 0x3000 to 0x3008.

It leaves R0, R3, SP, LR, condition flags
and the other general registers unchanged.
The next execution index is two bytes beyond
the instruction unless an exception intervenes.

The original AOSP expected assembler output
contains 0xC409 as an actual tested expected encoding.
That source records the encoding; it does not
record these hypothetical starting register values,
which have been supplied for this explicit example.

PART FIVE. WHAT THE BASE REGISTER MEANS FOR LDMIA.

Operation 58 similarly uses one low general-purpose
base register. Its 16-bit T32 encoding begins with
binary 11001, followed by Rn and an eight-bit mask.

Using the same initial base R4=0x3000, assume the
memory word at 0x3000 equals 0x11223344 and
the word at 0x3004 equals 0x55667788.
The actual halfword 0xCC09 corresponds to
LDMIA R4!, {R0,R3}. For normal execution,
R0 becomes 0x11223344, R3 becomes 0x55667788,
and R4 becomes 0x3008. No condition flags change.

Again, the exact 0xCC09 instruction halfword
appears in the pinned AOSP assembler expectation.
The memory values and base register state in this
document are explicit model inputs, not measurements
of an ART process executing the instruction.

PART SIX. WHY THE BASE REGISTER MAY NOT BE IN THE LIST HERE.

The physical T32 ISA has special base-in-list cases.

For the 16-bit LDMIA T1 encoding, including Rn in
the destination register list changes writeback
behavior: a form that loads Rn does not also write
the ordinary incremented end-address to that same
register as if the fields were independent.

For the 16-bit STMIA T1 encoding, base overlap
can make a stored base value unknown or impose
additional architecture-specific restrictions
depending on which register is selected first.

The first English Major C grant does NOT paper
over these subtleties. Operations 57 and 58
explicitly reject every overlapping-base register
list, leaving exact legal overlap forms for separate
new long names or a separately versioned profile.

The following cases are intentionally excluded:
  STMIA R4!, {R0,R4} under operation 57;
  LDMIA R4!, {R4} under operation 58.
The first may have special or unpredictable base
storage effects. The second has different
native writeback semantics from operation 58.
The English language does not replace such effects
with the behavior that would have been convenient.

PART SEVEN. CHANGE SP WITHOUT READING OR WRITING MEMORY.

Operation 59 advances SP by four times a seven-bit
unsigned immediate. The instruction halfword
has base hexadecimal 0xB000. Its integer
literal is an unscaled seven-bit value in the
encoded bits, but its semantic BYTE displacement
is that integer multiplied by four.

Operation 60 subtracts the same byte displacement
from SP. Its halfword base is 0xB080.
Both forms are TWO bytes long and preserve flags.

In pinned AOSP disassembly, 0xB014 means
advance SP by 80 bytes. The low seven bits
represent 20; multiplying by four gives 80.
0xB094 means subtract 80 bytes from SP.
These do not refer to 80 stack words.

Assume initial SP=0x2000.
ADD SP,#80 makes SP=0x2050 without writing
any memory. SUB SP,#80 subsequently
makes SP=0x2000, again without reading
or writing any data memory. That arithmetic
alone does not prove that either address
is inside a valid mapped user stack.

PART EIGHT. STACK ALIGNMENT AND CALLING CONVENTIONS.

A successful word-oriented PUSH/POP/LDMIA/STMIA
needs the applicable word-alignment requirements.
A higher-level function-call ABI can impose
additional constraints at public call boundaries,
including eight-byte SP alignment in the ARM
procedure-call standard (AAPCS).

These are distinct:
  instruction encoding legality;
  address alignment for each memory operation;
  memory accessibility of the whole transferred region;
  operating system's process stack mapping;
  ABI-mandated SP value at a function interface.

A source-language compiler must preserve ALL
declared obligations. It must not infer "safe
stack" merely from the existence of the
architectural SP register.

For example, starting SP aligned to eight bytes,
PUSH of three words subtracts twelve bytes,
leaving SP only four-byte aligned. That can be
a legal intermediate processor state even
though a later external call may require
realigning SP under the applicable ABI.

PART NINE. FAULTS AND INTERLEAVING ARE NOT IGNORED.

A load/store multiple can access more than one word.
One of those addresses may fault even if earlier
ones are accessible. The full architectural and
OS exception model determines which accesses and
register effects are observable after that fault.

The successful-transformation equations above
are NOT a rollback guarantee. They may not be used
to claim that an exception automatically undoes
earlier stores, or that all register results and
writeback are well defined after a mid-transfer
fault. Each exact encoding must have its
exception and restart semantics verified.

Nor may an application assume that two 32-bit
words written by one STMIA are one atomic
64-bit transaction, or that another observer
can never see the first word before the second.
A language with concurrency needs explicit
memory ordering and atomicity contracts.

Instruction fetch also remains distinct.
A POP loading PC uses the last WORD OF DATA
to select a code address; that word is not
itself executed as a T32 instruction.
PUSH storing LR writes return-address DATA,
not executable code.

PART TEN. EXPLICIT ALTERNATIVES RETAINED.

Alternative A: always lower a multi-register source
operation into several separate single-word LDR/STR
instructions. This may make each access explicit and
simplify some translation stages. It may increase
instruction count, and fault/interrupt/writeback
and observation effects must be compared before
calling the translation equivalent.

Alternative B: emit a native narrow PUSH/POP
or LDMIA/STMIA where legal. This can be concise
and is directly tied to the pinned machine encoding.
It leaves instruction-specific exceptional behavior
and register-list restrictions to be specified.

Alternative C: permit the native T1 base-overlap
special cases with separate precise English
semantics. This covers more real disassemblies,
but requires representing writeback-suppressed
loads and constrained/unpredictable store cases.
Those cases remain excluded by 57 and 58.

Alternative D: use 32-bit Thumb wide multi-register
instructions with higher registers, optional LR/PC
and explicit writeback controls. This expands the
source profile and is necessary for many real
compiled functions. The long operation name must
say which allowed variants and effects apply;
a blanket "same as narrow" assumption is wrong.

Alternative E: use dedicated SP +/- immediate
instead of PUSH/POP to reserve space for locals.
This changes SP but does not save/restores any
register values. It therefore cannot replace
a register-preserving prologue without additional
explicit memory transfers.

Alternative F: use a non-standard stack model
(for example, ascending or empty-stack conventions).
The ISA has other addressing modes, but a typical
ARM Android procedure call ABI uses full descending
stacks. This first grant is not an authorization to
interchange conventions across a call boundary.

All six possibilities remain recorded. The initial
narrow operations are admitted so that actual
machine bytes can be translated, not because
they have been proved optimal.

PART ELEVEN. SPECIFICATION-LEVEL ACCEPTANCE CASES.

Case 1: Given old SP 0x1000 and the three
registers R0/R3/LR from Part Two, expect
the three original values at 0x0FF4,
0x0FF8, 0x0FFC and new SP 0x0FF4.

Case 2: Given old SP 0x0FF4 and memory from
Part Three, POP {R0,R3,PC} restores the
two low registers, sets SP 0x1000
and selects T32 target 0x2000.

Case 3: Given R4 0x3000, R0 0x11223344,
R3 0x55667788, decode 0xC409 and expect
two word stores and R4 0x3008.

Case 4: Given R4 0x3000 and the two memory
words, decode 0xCC09 and expect R0, R3
loads and R4 0x3008.

Case 5: Decode 0xB014 and 0xB094 separately.
With SP 0x2000, the arithmetic results
are 0x2050 and 0x1FB0 respectively.
For a SEQUENCE in which 0xB094 follows
0xB014 starting at 0x2000, the final
SP is 0x2000. Flags remain unchanged.

Case 6: Reject a narrow PUSH with no low
register selected and M=0. Reject a
narrow POP with no selected destination
and P=0. A list containing LR only
for PUSH and a list containing PC only
for POP are nonempty lists, not
the same as an empty selection.

Case 7: Reject SP, LR or PC as the base
for operation 57 or 58. Reject any
list with a register above R7 for
these restricted 16-bit encodings.

Case 8: Reject Rn in the register list
for operations 57 and 58 even when
the physical ISA has a documented
nonmatching special case.

Case 9: Reject a claimed POP-to-PC
return when the loaded state-tag bit
requests A32 under this T32-only grant.

Case 10: Reject a claimed memory write
caused solely by ADD or SUB SP,#immediate.

Case 11: Reject the claim that
PUSH of three words preserves eight-byte
SP alignment merely because the
original SP was eight-byte aligned.

Case 12: Reject a claim that two word
stores caused by one STMIA form one
atomic eight-byte store without a
supported architecture/environment
atomicity guarantee.

Case 13: Record any mid-transfer fault
according to the exact architecture.
Do not silently mark it a successful
complete load/store or claim a rollback
that has not been specified.

These are proposed semantic fixtures,
not already executed acceptance results.
